## Pipeline

[`{{ d }}sources.toml`]({{ blob }}{{ d }}sources.toml) declares every input: its URL, its reuse
terms, and whether the pipeline reads it yet (`status`). `just fetch` downloads each
implemented source to `{{ d }}data/raw/` with a fetch record. `just build` writes
`{{ d }}data/processed/`. `just validate` checks the published output against the fetch
records. Neither directory goes into git. [ATTRIBUTION.md]({{ blob }}ATTRIBUTION.md) lists what
this repository reuses, and on what terms.

Commit `{{ d }}data/fetches.jsonl` after a fetch that changes it: it holds checksums, not
data, and is the history of every version of each source. Once a week, CI fetches again
and comments on a "Source data revised upstream" issue when a source changed, so keep
Issues turned on for this repository.
{% endif %}{% if 'ts' in stacks and ts_kind == 'web-app' %}{% set d = '' if ts_dir == '.' else ts_dir ~ '/' %}
## Deploy

`pnpm{% if ts_dir != '.' %} -C {{ ts_dir }}{% endif %} dev` serves the app with live reload. The app runs on a Cloudflare Worker
that serves the build as static assets{% if worker %}, with [`{{ d }}worker/index.ts`]({{ blob }}{{ d }}worker/index.ts) in front of `/api/*`{% endif %}, configured in [`{{ d }}wrangler.jsonc`]({{ blob }}{{ d }}wrangler.jsonc). CI deploys it: every
push to `main` deploys the build the checks ran on, at `{{ site_url }}`. Every pull request that is
neither a draft nor from a bot gets a preview URL (see below). [`{{ d }}public/_headers`]({{ blob }}{{ d }}public/_headers) sets the
response headers, including the Content-Security-Policy that the e2e tests run under.

One-time setup, for production and previews alike:

1. Run `just repo-settings` to create the `production` and `preview` environments, or create them under Settings > Environments, with `production` limited to `main`.
2. Add the secrets `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` (a custom token with Account > Workers Scripts: Edit{% if custom_domain %}, and Zone > Workers Routes: Edit for the zone of `{{ custom_domain }}`{% endif %}) and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` to both environments.
3. Give the Cloudflare account a workers.dev subdomain, if it has none yet.

Without the token, the deploy jobs skip with a notice. {% if custom_domain %}The production job attaches the custom domain `{{ custom_domain }}`, whose zone must be in the same Cloudflare account. {% endif %}For {% if custom_domain %}another{% else %}a{% endif %} custom domain, set `site_url` with `copier update --data site_url=https://<domain>`.{% if worker %} `pnpm{% if ts_dir != '.' %} -C {{ ts_dir }}{% endif %} exec wrangler dev` serves the last build and the Worker together.{% endif %}

